# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env.
#
# NOTE: There are deliberately NO LinkedIn credentials in this file, and there
# never will be. The system does not log into LinkedIn — see the compliance
# rules in README.md.
# It reads LinkedIn's *email notifications* out of David's Gmail instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI providers. config.yaml picks which one drafting and chat each use, and
# you only need the key for the provider(s) you actually named there.
#
#   drafting.provider / chat.provider = "gemini" -> GEMINI_API_KEY
#   drafting.provider / chat.provider = "claude" -> ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
#   ... = "mock"   -> no key needed, nothing leaves the server
#
# Gemini key: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GEMINI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gmail API — READ-ONLY (scope: gmail.readonly).
#
# IMPORTANT — we do NOT read David's personal mailbox. He auto-forwards a copy
# of LinkedIn's notification emails to an alias on the developer's Google
# Workspace mailbox, and that is what gets read. Three reasons:
#
#   1. Permanence. An OAuth app whose Audience is "Internal" (Workspace-only)
#      never expires its refresh tokens and never needs Google verification.
#      An "External" app left in Testing expires them every 7 days, and
#      publishing out of Testing with gmail.readonly — a restricted scope —
#      triggers OAuth verification.
#   2. Privacy. The server can only ever see mail David chose to forward,
#      never the rest of his inbox.
#   3. Revocability. He deletes one filter and it stops, on his own account,
#      without involving us.
#
# SETUP — forwarding into an alias on the developer's Workspace mailbox
#
# Below, ALIAS is the forwarding alias on that mailbox (referred to in
# config.yaml's gmail_query by the label applied in step 4).
#
# 1. Workspace admin > the developer's user > Alternate emails. Add ALIAS.
#    An alias is free; it needs no extra seat and lands in that same mailbox.
#
# 2. David verifies the alias — Gmail > Settings > Forwarding and POP/IMAP >
#    "Add a forwarding address". Gmail mails a confirmation link to ALIAS,
#    which lands in the developer's mailbox; clicking it verifies the address
#    on DAVID's account. So do step 1 before he starts.
#
#    ⚠️  He must then STOP. Do NOT select "Forward a copy of incoming mail
#    to" on that screen — that forwards his ENTIRE inbox, personal mail
#    included. Verifying the address is all that screen is for here.
#
# 3. David creates the filter — Gmail > Settings > Filters > new filter:
#      From:    linkedin.com
#      Action:  Forward it to ALIAS
#    The filter is what actually forwards, and only LinkedIn mail matches it.
#    The address only appears in that dropdown once step 2 is verified.
#    This sends a COPY — David's originals stay in his own inbox, unchanged.
#
# 4. In the receiving mailbox's own Gmail — a second filter:
#      Matches:  deliveredto:ALIAS
#      Do this:  Apply label "davids-linkedin", Skip the Inbox
#    The label is what config.yaml's gmail_query matches. Without it, the
#    query would also pick up that mailbox's own LinkedIn mail — see the
#    warning in config.yaml > inbox.
#
# 5. In Google Cloud Console:
#      enable the Gmail API
#      Google Auth Platform > Audience    -> INTERNAL (requires Workspace,
#                                            which is what makes this offered)
#      Google Auth Platform > Data Access -> add .../auth/gmail.readonly
#      Google Auth Platform > Clients     -> Create client, type "Desktop app"
#        ("Desktop app" needs no redirect URI. With "Web application" instead,
#         register http://localhost:8765/ against it.)
#
# 6. Signed in as the Workspace user — NOT the alias, NOT David:
#      .venv/bin/python scripts/get_gmail_token.py
#      .venv/bin/python scripts/check_gmail.py
#
# WHICH MAILBOX GETS READ is decided entirely by whose account authorized
# GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN in step 6. The API calls use userId="me". GMAIL_USER is
# required but never read by the code — set it to the same address for the
# sake of whoever reads this .env later, but changing it changes nothing.
#
# NOTE ON SCOPE: gmail.readonly is per-mailbox, not per-label. This token can
# read all mail in that mailbox, not just the davids-linkedin label. The code
# only ever queries that label. A dedicated Workspace seat (~$7/mo) is the way
# to shrink that blast radius if it matters.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=
GMAIL_USER=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Buffer — the ONLY sanctioned automation touchpoint: scheduled posting via an
# official LinkedIn API partner, and only after David approves the text.
# BUFFER_PROFILE_ID identifies David's LinkedIn profile inside Buffer.
#
# OPEN DECISION: Buffer vs. another official partner is not settled.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUFFER_TOKEN=
BUFFER_PROFILE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Twilio — the texts David gets, and the replies that come back.
#
# Outbound: approval requests ("Here's your post... reply YES"), plus the
# nudges configured under `alerts` in config.yaml.
#
# INBOUND NEEDS ONE THING SET IN THE TWILIO CONSOLE. Without it, David's "YES"
# goes nowhere and the text is notify-only — he'd have to open the dashboard to
# approve. Phone Numbers > (your number) > Messaging > "A MESSAGE COMES IN":
#
#     Webhook   POST   https://<your-railway-domain>/sms/incoming
#
# That URL must match exactly what the service is reachable at. Twilio signs
# the URL it called, and the endpoint recomputes that signature — a mismatch
# (http vs https, a trailing slash, the wrong domain) fails verification and
# every reply is silently rejected with a 403. The server logs a line pointing
# at this when that happens.
#
# SMS_AUTH_TOKEN is what verifies inbound requests, not just what authorises
# outbound ones. If it is unset, /sms/incoming refuses everything rather than
# trusting an unverifiable request — that endpoint can publish under David's
# name, so it fails closed by design.
#
# David's number itself lives in config.yaml under `approvals.phone_number`,
# not here — it is configuration, not a secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SMS_ACCOUNT_SID=
SMS_AUTH_TOKEN=
SMS_FROM_NUMBER=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dashboard — David's 4-digit PIN login. No password he has to remember.
# Change this before David ever sees the URL.
#
# SESSION_SECRET signs his login cookie. Use a long random string, and keep it
# STABLE across deploys — changing it signs him out.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DASHBOARD_PIN=0000
SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime / deploy
# DATABASE_PATH: on Railway/Render this points at the mounted persistent
# volume, e.g. /data/david.db. Locally it defaults to ./data/david.db.
# CONFIG_PATH: override only if config.yaml lives somewhere unusual.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_PATH=
CONFIG_PATH=
